<div class="link-overview">
  <section class="link-problem" aria-labelledby="predicting-a-link">
    <div class="problem-copy">
      <p class="eyebrow">Last.fm Asia · Social graph</p>
      <h2 id="predicting-a-link">
        Who is missing
        <br />a <em>connection?</em>
      </h2>
      <p>
        Users are nodes; friendships are edges. Hide some known friendships,
        then ask the model to rank those pairs above sampled unconnected pairs.
        This is the idea behind <strong>“people you may know.”</strong>
      </p>
      <dl class="graph-facts">
        <div>
          <dt>Users</dt>
          <dd>7,624</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Friendships</dt>
          <dd>27,806</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Graph density</dt>
          <dd>0.096%</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
      <p class="small-note">
        Very few of all possible user pairs are connected.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure>
      <img
        src="/media/link-prediction/neighbourhood.svg"
        width="720"
        height="490"
        alt="A real 13-user Last.fm subgraph. Two users, u and v, are highlighted; their friendship is dashed to illustrate a link hidden for prediction."
      />
      <figcaption>
        Real network excerpt; hiding the dashed edge is an illustration, not a
        model prediction.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="link-models" aria-labelledby="link-prediction-models">
    <header class="section-heading">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">The modeling comparison</p>
        <h2 id="link-prediction-models">
          Learn a vector.
          <br />
          Or learn from neighbours.
        </h2>
      </div>
      <p>
        <strong>Matrix factorization</strong> learns a vector for each user
        directly. The <strong>graph convolutional network (GCN)</strong> builds
        representations by combining node features with information from
        connected users.
      </p>
    </header>
    <figure class="architecture-figure">
      <picture>
        <source
          media="(max-width: 700px)"
          srcset="/media/link-prediction/architecture-mobile.svg"
        />
        <img
          src="/media/link-prediction/architecture.svg"
          width="1180"
          height="460"
          loading="lazy"
          alt="Two model architectures. Matrix factorization maps user IDs to 10-dimensional vectors and scores a dot product; batch gradient descent updates the vectors from squared error and L2 regularization. The GCN aggregates neighbours through three layers into 256-dimensional vectors; an MLP scores pair features. Binary cross-entropy and Adam update both networks."
        />
      </picture>
    </figure>
    <div class="objective-pair">
      <div>
        <h3>The optimization baseline</h3>
        <div
          class="formula"
          aria-label="Minimize over U the sum of squared errors between labels and vector dot products, plus lambda times the squared Frobenius norm of U."
        >
          <math display="block"><munder><mo>min</mo><mi>U</mi></munder><mrow><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>∈</mo><mi>Ω</mi></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>λ</mi><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></math>
        </div>
        <p>
          <strong>U</strong> holds the user vectors. Training pairs have labels
          1 or 0; <strong>λ</strong> limits vector magnitude. NumPy accumulates
          each pair’s gradient into both users’ vectors.
        </p>
        <span class="config">Learning rate 0.01 · λ = 0.01</span>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>The neural edge scorer</h3>
        <div class="pair-features">
          <span>zᵢ</span>
          <span>zⱼ</span>
          <span>zᵢ ⊙ zⱼ</span>
          <span>|zᵢ − zⱼ|</span>
          <b aria-hidden="true">→</b>
          <strong>MLP</strong>
        </div>
        <p>
          The decoder combines both vectors, their elementwise product and their
          absolute difference. It can learn interactions beyond a single dot
          product.
        </p>
        <span class="config">
          Adam · learning rate 0.001 · weight decay 0.00005
        </span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="link-results" aria-labelledby="link-prediction-results">
    <header class="section-heading">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Evidence from the project</p>
        <h2 id="link-prediction-results">
          Fit the graph.
          <br />
          Then test the ranking.
        </h2>
      </div>
      <p>
        <strong>ROC-AUC</strong> measures how often a positive pair ranks above
        a sampled negative. It evaluates ranking, without choosing a
        classification threshold.
      </p>
    </header>
    <div class="result-grid">
      <figure>
        <h3>Held-out link ranking</h3>
        <picture>
          <source
            media="(max-width: 700px)"
            srcset="/media/link-prediction/auc-mobile.svg"
          />
          <img
            src="/media/link-prediction/auc.svg"
            width="640"
            height="360"
            loading="lazy"
            alt="Reported test ROC-AUC: matrix factorization 0.8435 and GCN plus MLP 0.9579. Random ranking is 0.5. The two experiments used different test sets."
          />
        </picture>
        <figcaption>
          Report Tables 1–2. Baseline: 11,124 test pairs; GCN: 5,560. Both use
          1:1 positive/negative sampling.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <h3>Baseline training objective</h3>
        <picture>
          <source
            media="(max-width: 700px)"
            srcset="/media/link-prediction/convergence-mobile.svg"
          />
          <img
            src="/media/link-prediction/convergence.svg"
            width="640"
            height="360"
            loading="lazy"
            alt="Ten saved loss values decrease from 22,288.6142 at epoch zero to 2,111.2992 at epoch 45. Points are connected without smoothing."
          />
        </picture>
        <figcaption>
          Redrawn from ten logged checkpoints. The original full curve has a
          small rebound; a falling loss does not prove global optimality.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
    <div class="result-takeaway">
      <p>
        <strong>GCN + MLP achieved the higher reported ranking score</strong>,
        with average precision 0.9655. The baseline recovered many positives
        (recall 0.9087), with lower precision (0.6061).
      </p>
      <p>
        These are separate reported runs: different splits and embedding sizes
        mean the AUC gap is{' '}
        <strong>not a controlled measure of the architecture alone</strong>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</div>

